Interoperability
----------------
StitchCraft reads and writes the embroidery parameters used by Ink/Stitch (https://inkstitch.org) as
SVG attributes. Parameter names, meanings and defaults are used for file compatibility only. StitchCraft
contains no Ink/Stitch source code, documentation text, images, fonts or data files; Ink/Stitch is
licensed under the GNU GPL v3.

Third-party data
----------------
Entries are added here when a data table is derived from a third-party source.

- Brother PEC thread palette (crates/stitchcraft-plan/src/palette/brother_pec.rs): index, name and RGB
  of the 64 PEC colours as published in pyembroidery (https://github.com/EmbroidePy/pyembroidery, MIT
  License; author Tatarize). Entries 62 and 63, appliqué functions on Brother machines, follow its fork
  pystitch (https://github.com/inkstitch/pystitch, MIT License, main branch at commit b72b557), where
  pyembroidery 1.5.1 lists two thread colours. No pyembroidery or pystitch code is included.
- PES/PEC and DST encoding conventions (crates/stitchcraft-formats): where public format descriptions
  leave a detail open (trim flags, header fields, thumbnail bit order, DST trim sequences), StitchCraft
  matches the output of pystitch (MIT), observed as a black box: we ran the library and compared bytes.
  Each such detail is noted in the module that writes it. How machines read DST jumps — three or more
  in a row cut the thread — comes from public descriptions (Brother's support pages, Wilcom's
  documentation, the EduTech Wiki's DST page) and agrees with pyembroidery's reader (MIT), observed the
  same way.
- Tartan colour shades (crates/stitchcraft-engine/src/generators/tartan/shades.rs): for each of 25 colour
  letters, the name and value of one of its shades, chosen from "Tartan Register colours"
  (https://www.tartanregister.gov.uk/docs/Colour_shades.pdf). Crown copyright; source: the Scottish
  Register of Tartans, "Tartan Register colours". Re-used free of charge, as the register's copyright terms
  allow for its website's material (https://www.tartanregister.gov.uk/copyright).

Third-party models
------------------
The subject cut-out (`stitch plan --subject`, `stitch cutout`; apps/stitchcraft-cutout) runs 2 models that
it fetches on first use from StitchCraft's release cutout-models-1, which publishes them with their licences,
a notice of their own and their export record. The repository holds only their pins
(apps/stitchcraft-cutout/models/models.toml), and no part of either model.

- U²-Net-p, the small model of U²-Net, by Xuebin Qin, Zichen Zhang, Chenyang Huang, Masood Dehghan, Osmar
  Zaiane and Martin Jagersand (https://github.com/xuebinqin/U-2-Net), under the Apache License 2.0, which its
  repository is under and which its author applies to its models (its issue 208). The file is rembg's ONNX
  conversion of the author's checkpoint (https://github.com/danielgatis/rembg, MIT License, Copyright (c)
  2020 Daniel Gatis), as rembg publishes it.
- MobileSAM, by Chaoning Zhang, Dongshen Han, Yu Qiao, Jung Uk Kim, Sung-Ho Bae, Seungkyu Lee and Choong Seon
  Hong (https://github.com/ChaoningZhang/MobileSAM), distilled from Meta AI's Segment Anything, under the
  Apache License 2.0, which covers its weights. StitchCraft exported its encoder and its decoder to ONNX
  from the checkpoint in its repository, with apps/stitchcraft-cutout/models/export.py.

